## 0. NIST FIPS 203 — ML-KEM-768 Parameter Set

The incremental ML-KEM-768 layer implements the ML-KEM-768 parameter
set of FIPS 203 (Section 8, Tables 2–3). All byte-length constants in
`src/incremental_mlkem768.rs` are derivable from these parameters.

### 0a. ML-KEM-768 Parameters (FIPS 203, Table 2)

| Symbol | Value | Role |
|--------|-------|------|
| n | 256 | Polynomial degree (constant across all parameter sets) |
| q | 3329 | Modulus (constant across all parameter sets; q = 2⁸·13 + 1) |
| k | 3 | Module rank — determines matrix/vector dimensions |
| η₁ | 2 | CBD parameter for secret/error vectors (KeyGen, Encrypt) |
| η₂ | 2 | CBD parameter for encryption noise (Encrypt) |
| dᵤ | 10 | Compression parameter for ciphertext component c₁ |
| dᵥ | 4 | Compression parameter for ciphertext component c₂ |
| RBG strength | ≥ 192 bits | Required randomness-generation security strength |
| Security category | 3 | NIST security category (≥ AES-192 equivalent) |

### 0b. ML-KEM-768 Key and Ciphertext Sizes (FIPS 203, Table 3)

| Object | Formula (FIPS 203) | Bytes |
|--------|--------------------|-------|
| Encapsulation key (ek) | 384·k + 32 = 384·3 + 32 | **1184** |
| Decapsulation key (dk) | 768·k + 96 = 768·3 + 96 | **2400** |
| Ciphertext (c = c₁‖c₂) | 32·(dᵤ·k + dᵥ) = 32·(10·3 + 4) | **1088** |
| Shared secret key (K) | 32 | **32** |

### 0c. Ciphertext Decomposition

FIPS 203 §5.2 (Algorithm 14, K-PKE.Encrypt) defines the ciphertext as
the concatenation c = c₁‖c₂, where:

| Component | Formula | Bytes | Rust constant |
|-----------|---------|-------|---------------|
| c₁ | 32·dᵤ·k = 32·10·3 | **960** | `CIPHERTEXT1_SIZE` |
| c₂ | 32·dᵥ = 32·4 | **128** | `CIPHERTEXT2_SIZE` |
| c₁ + c₂ | 960 + 128 | **1088** | (total ciphertext) |

### 0d. Incremental Key Decomposition

The upstream `libcrux_ml_kem::mlkem768::incremental` API splits the
FIPS 203 keys as follows. The mapping to FIPS 203 entities is:

| Incremental API | FIPS 203 origin | Bytes | Rust constant |
|----------------|-----------------|-------|---------------|
| `pk1` (Header) | Seed ρ (32 bytes from K-PKE.KeyGen, Alg. 13 line 2) + hash H(ek) (32 bytes from ML-KEM.KeyGen_internal, Alg. 16 line 3) | **64** | `HEADER_SIZE` |
| `pk2` (EncapsulationKey) | ByteEncode₁₂(t̂) = first 384·k bytes of ek (FIPS 203 §5.1, Alg. 13 line 10) | **1152** | `ENCAPSULATION_KEY_SIZE` |
| `sk` (DecapsulationKey) | Full dk (FIPS 203 §6.1, Alg. 16 line 3): dk_PKE‖ek‖H(ek)‖z | **2400** | — |
| Encapsulation state | Internal libcrux state: r_as_ntt (3 × 512 = 1536 bytes) + error2 (512 bytes) + randomness (32 bytes) | **2080** | — |
| Shared secret | K = output of ML-KEM.Encaps / ML-KEM.Decaps (FIPS 203 §7.2–7.3) | **32** | — |

### 0e. Decapsulation Failure Rate (FIPS 203, Table 1)

| Parameter set | Failure rate |
|---------------|-------------|
| ML-KEM-768 | 2⁻¹⁶⁴·⁸ |

This is the probability that `K' ≠ K` under well-formed inputs and
successful randomness generation (FIPS 203 §3.2). In the extracted
model, `decapsulate_compressed_key` always returns a `default` 32-byte
array, so decapsulation failure is not modelled.

