## 6. Inter-Function Spec Theorems: FIPS 203 KEM Chain

FIPS 203 §3.1 (Figure 1) defines the canonical KEM protocol as a
three-step chain: **KeyGen → Encaps → Decaps**. The incremental
ML-KEM-768 API decomposes this into five callable functions
(`generate`, `ek_matches_header`, `encaps1`, `encaps2`, `decaps`).
The spec theorems in the Lean verification must not only prove each
function correct in isolation, but also establish that **the output
of each function satisfies the preconditions of the next function in
the chain**, ensuring composability.

This section enumerates the inter-function spec theorems that link
these functions together, grounded in FIPS 203.

### 6a. The FIPS 203 KEM Protocol Chain

```
FIPS 203 Figure 1 (§3.1):

  Alice                                    Bob
  ─────                                    ───
  (ek, dk) ← ML-KEM.KeyGen()              │
       │                                   │
       ├──── ek ────────────────────────────►
       │                                   │
       │                    (K, c) ← ML-KEM.Encaps(ek)
       │                                   │
       ◄──── c ────────────────────────────┤
       │                                   │
  K' ← ML-KEM.Decaps(dk, c)               │
       │                                   │
  assert K' = K                            │
```

In the incremental API, this becomes:

```
  Alice                                    Bob
  ─────                                    ───
  keys ← generate(rng)                    │
  hdr  = keys.hdr   (64 bytes)            │
  ek   = keys.ek    (1152 bytes)           │
  dk   = keys.dk    (2400 bytes)           │
       │                                   │
       ├──── hdr, ek ──────────────────────►
       │                                   │
       │              assert ek_matches_header(ek, hdr) = true
       │              (ct1, es, ss) ← encaps1(hdr, rng)
       │              ct2 ← encaps2(ek, es)
       │                                   │
       ◄──── ct1, ct2 ─────────────────────┤
       │                                   │
  ss' ← decaps(dk, ct1, ct2)              │
       │                                   │
  assert |ss'| = |ss| = 32                │
  (and K' = K, not verified — opaque)      │
```

### 6b. Existing Spec Theorems (per-function)

The following spec theorems are already proved in the Lean spec tree.
Each establishes byte-length postconditions for a single function:

